    "q-340511": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340511",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,6)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+12)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,6).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+12)=f((x+6)+6)=f(x+6)=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(7)=f(1+6)=f(1)=F(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7)=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+3)=f(5)=F(5)=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+3)\\ne f(x)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
