    "q-340681": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340681",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-340681-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
